#. translators: %s: site name
#: includes/class-dsar-shortcode.php:1648
#, php-format
msgid "[%s] A data request was refused by the per-connection limit"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: site name, 2: the per-IP ceiling, 3: admin URL of the stored requests list
#: includes/class-dsar-shortcode.php:1651
#, php-format
msgid ""
"%1$s refused a data subject request: the connection it came from had already sent %2$d in the last 24 hours.\n"
"\n"
"If that is a flood, nothing needs doing — the visitor was given an email fallback and the limit resets 24 hours after the first request in the window.\n"
"\n"
"If genuine requests are being refused, the usual cause is that every visitor resolves to the same address because the site sits behind a proxy whose forwarded header the plugin has not been told to read. What restores a per-visitor key depends on which proxy it is, and one line is enough:\n"
"\n"
"- A reverse proxy or cache on the same WordPress host, reached over loopback (nginx, Varnish): set faz_loopback_proxy_header to the one header that proxy writes — HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR if its config says proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for, HTTP_X_REAL_IP if it says proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr. Nothing is read from a loopback proxy until this is named, because a header it does not write arrives from the visitor untouched.\n"
"\n"
"- Any other front end the plugin sees over a private address — a proxy in a separate container (REMOTE_ADDR like 172.17.0.3), a load balancer on the LAN — or a CDN other than Cloudflare: add that peer's own range to faz_trusted_proxy_cidrs, for example 172.17.0.0/16 for a Docker bridge or the CDN's published edge ranges. faz_loopback_proxy_header will not reach it: it applies to loopback peers only. Adding the range is the whole change if the front end appends to X-Forwarded-For. If it overwrites a header of its own instead (True-Client-IP, Fastly-Client-IP), also set faz_proxy_forwarded_header to that header — the range alone will not do it.\n"
"\n"
"- Cloudflare: already handled, nothing to set.\n"
"\n"
"- Last resort, for a front end that overwrites the forwarded header rather than appending to it: enable faz_trust_proxy_headers and scope it with faz_trusted_proxies. This path reads the leftmost entry of the chain, so on a front end that appends it would hand the visitor control of the value — prefer faz_trusted_proxy_cidrs above, which reads the chain from the right.\n"
"\n"
"faz_dsar_max_per_ip_per_day changes the ceiling, and 0 switches it off.\n"
"\n"
"Stored requests: %3$s\n"
"\n"
"You will get this notice at most once an hour."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: site name, 2: requests in the last 24 hours, 3: the per-request mail ceiling, 4: requests awaiting action, 5: admin URL of the stored requests list
#: includes/class-dsar-shortcode.php:1908
#, php-format
msgid ""
"%1$s has received %2$d data subject requests in the last 24 hours, past the %3$d after which they are no longer emailed one by one. They are still being recorded: %4$d are awaiting action.\n"
"\n"
"Review them here: %5$s\n"
"\n"
"This usually means the form is being targeted by automated submissions. Install a form-protection plugin (ALTCHA / OpenPorte, Turnstile, hCaptcha) and hook it into the faz_dsar_pre_submit filter, so the genuine requests are emailed individually again.\n"
"\n"
"You will get this summary at most once an hour while it lasts."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-dsar-shortcode.php:1917
msgid "One limit is not helping here: the per-connection ceiling is switched off on this site because every visitor resolves to the same address, which happens behind a proxy whose forwarded header the plugin has not been told to read. It is deliberately skipped rather than applied, since applying it would refuse unrelated people's requests for 24 hours. What gets it back depends on which proxy sits in front, and it is one line either way: for a reverse proxy or cache on this same host, reached over loopback, set faz_loopback_proxy_header to the single header that proxy writes (HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR for proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for, HTTP_X_REAL_IP for proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr) — nothing is read from it until that header is named, because one it does not write arrives from the visitor untouched; for any front end the plugin sees over a private address instead, such as a proxy in a separate container or a load balancer on the LAN, and for a CDN other than Cloudflare, add that peer's own range to faz_trusted_proxy_cidrs (172.17.0.0/16 for a Docker bridge, or the CDN's published edge ranges), since faz_loopback_proxy_header applies to loopback peers only, and if that front end overwrites a header of its own rather than appending to X-Forwarded-For, also name it in faz_proxy_forwarded_header, because the range alone will not do it; faz_trust_proxy_headers with faz_trusted_proxies remains available but reads the leftmost entry of the chain, so prefer the range above, which reads it from the right. Cloudflare needs nothing."
msgstr ""

